It defines:
- the **types** of messages, e.g. request and response;
- the **syntax**: which fields a message has and how they are separated;
- the **semantics**: what the fields mean;
- the **rules** for when and how processes send and answer messages.
An **open** protocol is published in RFCs (Requests for Comments), so anyone can build to it and products from different makers can work together, e.g. HTTP, SMTP. A **proprietary** protocol is owned by a company and not public, e.g. Zoom, Skype.
Key: open protocols make interoperability possible.
